course: chinese-street
lesson: 6
title: Counting 1 to 10, on one hand
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ming counts to ten while you hide: yī, èr, sān, sì, wǔ, liù, qī, bā, jiǔ, shí! You learn it by ear, the way kids pick up a counting rhyme.
explain: Chinese hands count differently too. All the numbers up to ten fit on one hand: six is thumb and little finger out like a surfer's «hang loose», eight is an L like a toy gun, and nine is a hooked finger like a little question mark.
explain: Some numbers bring luck because of what they sound like: bā (8) rhymes with fā (get rich), and sì (4) sounds like sǐ (die), so many families avoid it in house numbers and gifts.
explain: Two tricky bits: for «two of something» you say liǎng, not èr; and on the phone people read 1 as yāo so it can't be confused with qī (7).
fact: yī («ee», high) is one; it changes tone before other words, as in yí ge (one of) and yì tiān (one day).
fact: èr («ar!» like a pirate, falling) is two when counting; for two of something you say liǎng.
fact: sān («sahn», high) is three; on Chinese hands three is usually index, middle and ring finger, not the British thumb and two.
fact: sì («sz!», falling) is four; it sounds like sǐ (to die), so 4 is unlucky for many families in phone numbers, houses and gifts of four.
fact: wǔ («woo», dipping) is five, five fingers spread.
fact: liù («lyo!», falling) is six, shown with thumb and little finger out; it is lucky because it sounds like «going smoothly», and 666 in games means «you're so good!».
fact: qī («chee», high) is seven, shown with the fingertips pinched together; rising qí means to ride.
fact: bā («bah», high) is eight, the luckiest number because it rhymes with fā (to get rich); shown as an L of thumb and index finger, which is not pointing a gun at anyone.
fact: jiǔ («jyo», dipping) is nine, shown as a hooked index finger; it sounds like the word for long-lasting, so it is lucky too.
fact: shí («shr?», rising, tongue curled back) is ten, shown with crossed index fingers or a fist; dipping shǐ is poo.
fact: liǎng («lyahng», dipping) is two of something: liǎng ge rén (two people), liǎng kuài qián (two pounds); «èr ge» is a classic slip.
fact: yāo («yow», high) is one when reading out phone, room or bus numbers digit by digit, so it isn't mixed up with qī (seven).
exercise: How do you say three? | sān | sì | shí | èr
exercise: What does sì mean? | Four | Ten | Seven | Six
exercise: Why do many families avoid the number 4? | sì sounds like sǐ, to die | It is an odd number | It is the emperor's number | It sounds like «poo»
exercise: Which number sounds like a pirate's «ar!»? | èr, two | bā, eight | yī, one | liù, six
exercise: How many is wǔ? | Five | Nine | Two | Four
exercise: Ming holds out his thumb and little finger. What number is it? | Six | Two | Eight | Ten
exercise: What does 666 mean in a game chat? | You're so good! | You're so evil! | Game over, you lost | Six more lives left
exercise: What is qī? | Seven | One | Ten | Four
exercise: Why is bā the luckiest number? | It rhymes with fā, to get rich | It looks like a heart on its side | It was the emperor's lucky age | It sounds like the word for dad
exercise: Ming makes an L with his thumb and index finger. What does it mean? | Eight | A gun, he's threatening you | Loser | Seven
exercise: A hooked index finger like a question mark is | Nine | One | Six | Ten
exercise: Which tone does ten, shí, have? | Rising | Dipping | High and level | Falling
exercise: Two people is | liǎng ge rén | èr ge rén | èr liǎng rén | yī yī rén
exercise: What is yāo? | One, when reading out phone or room numbers | A special number between one and two | The word for zero when reading out numbers | Seven, when reading out bus numbers
exercise: Why do people say yāo on the phone? | So one isn't mixed up with qī, seven | Because yī is a rude word on the phone | Because yāo is lucky for phone calls | Because it is shorter than one
exercise: Why does jiǔ, nine, count as lucky? | It sounds like long-lasting | It rhymes with fā, «get rich» | It is the last number | It looks like a little dragon
exercise: You hear «yí ge» and «yì tiān». What happened to yī? | Its tone changed before other words | It turned into liǎng, two of something | It became a different number | Nobody knows
test: «ming's showing me a gun with his fingers, rude!!» He was counting. What number? | Eight | Two | Ten | Five
test: «èr ge píngguǒ please» — Ming's sister giggles. What should it be? | liǎng ge píngguǒ | sì ge píngguǒ | èr èr píngguǒ | liǎng liǎng píngguǒ
test: Ming reads his phone number: «yāo sān bā ...». What digit is yāo? | One | Seven | Zero | Eight
test: What would happen if you gave Ming's grandparents a gift of four pears? | Some might see it as unlucky, because of sì | They would think it very lucky and say thanks | It means «I love you» | Nothing, four is the luckiest number
test: Counting «bā, jiǔ, shǐ!» What did you say last? | Poo, not ten | Ten, correctly | Eleven | Nine again
test: In a cartoon a British kid shows three with thumb, index and middle finger in China. Is it wrong? | Understood, but Chinese kids use index, middle and ring | Yes, in China that hand sign is a rude word | Yes, it means eight | No, every country counts to three on hands the same way
test: «bloody lift, our flat's on floor 4 and nan won't take it» Why might she dislike 4? | sì sounds like the word for death | 4 means «poo» in Chinese | 4 is too high up for her to climb | Floor 4 is the emperor's floor
test: Ming says «liù liù liù!» after you score. What does he mean? | You're so good! | You're cheating! | Six goals to me! | Game over!
test: A hand with fingertips pinched together means | Seven | Zero | Ten | Bird
test: Which counting is right? | yī, èr, sān, sì, wǔ | yī, èr, sì, sān, wǔ | yāo, liǎng, sān, sì, wǔ | yī, èr, sān, shí, wǔ
exam: Identify the numeral employed for «two» when it precedes a measure word, as in «two persons». | liǎng | èr | yāo | shí
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the numeral sì is regarded by many families as inauspicious. | Its pronunciation resembles the verb signifying «to die» | It was the number reserved for the emperor | It is regarded as an odd number and therefore unfortunate | It resembles the word for excrement
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of yāo. | It denotes the digit one when numbers are read digit by digit | It denotes the numeral seven when telephone numbers are read aloud | It is the numeral zero | It denotes two of an object
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hand sign formed by the extended thumb and index finger? | It denotes the numeral eight | It constitutes a threatening gesture | It denotes the numeral two | It signifies defeat in a game
